he following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006797/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada6e26-3e25-5024-8918-75ece9936f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109848} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c31b7-948e-5ad7-b087-0fe89630298e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109849} {:answer/id #uuid "4c34414e-2009-5cf3-b56c-4690e524521f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109850} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4d65ad-f84e-57b5-af67-61b03c8dcd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109851} {:answer/id #uuid "793ba2fd-9d15-5423-bdbe-c7924d65df92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "793ba2fd-9d15-5423-bdbe-c7924d65df92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109852} :answer-field/id #uuid "b398f04b-0e66-5616-855b-c595b649c5d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7680c22b-fa61-58ad-8d65-6ff8999855b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-65947" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}]}
